-- A mixfix rule such as `p `is eligible`` is one definition, found by its word.
-- A different rule may still have an input named like that word: `decision`
-- below takes one input, called `is eligible`, so the line that defines it is
-- an ordinary rule, not a line written like the mixfix rule. It was refused
-- as if it were (found by the review of legalese/l4-ide#545, round 4).
-- The calls are written head first: main's exact-printer reorders an infix
-- mixfix call (smucclaw/l4-ide#918, fixed on unstable), which the .ep.golden
-- would then record.
DECLARE Person IS ONE OF Alice, Bob

GIVEN p IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
p `is eligible` MEANS p EQUALS Alice

GIVEN `is eligible` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A STRING
decision `is eligible` MEANS IF `is eligible` THEN "yes" ELSE "no"

#EVAL `is eligible` Alice    -- TRUE
#EVAL decision TRUE          -- "yes"
#EVAL decision FALSE         -- "no"
